#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide shows how to use the [SBD "
"eigensolver](https://github.com/Qiskit/sbd-eigensolver-python) as an "
"alternative selected configuration interaction (SCI) solver to diagonalize "
"larger fermionic problems beyond what the default PySCF solver can support. "
"SBD (Selected Basis Diagonalization) runs in-process, is parallelized with "
"MPI, and offers both CPU and CUDA backends. For information on how to "
"install and use it, see the [SBD "
"documentation](https://github.com/Qiskit/sbd-eigensolver-python).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SBD compiles a C++ extension against the MPI and BLAS libraries available on"
" the machine, so you must install both before you install the `sbd` extra:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because `solve_sci_batch` already matches the solver interface that "
"`diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian` expects, you can pass it directly to "
"`sci_solver` after you bind its configuration options with "
"`functools.partial`. To run on a CUDA-capable GPU instead of the CPU, pass "
"`device_config=DeviceConfig.gpu()` from `sbd.device_config`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For more details on the SQD code used in this example, see the [chemistry "
"Hamiltonian tutorial](/tutorials/sample-based-quantum-diagonalization).\n"
msgstr ""
